<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Óscar Carmona</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1869–1951</span> · Also known as António Óscar Carmona</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Óscar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmona">Carmona</a> was a Portuguese army officer who took part in the coup of 28 May 1926 and soon pushed aside Gomes da Costa to lead the military dictatorship. He was president of Portugal from 1926 until his death in 1951 and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-oliveira-salazar">António de Oliveira Salazar</a> into government, appointing him prime minister in 1932 and serving as head of state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oscar-carmona.jpg" alt="Óscar Carmona" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manuel Alves de San Payo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fotografia_oficial_do_Presidente_da_Rep%C3%BAblica_%C3%93scar_Carmona_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
